
(verb :name "error-object-type" :std "Motoi" :library "exception" :tier "core" :kind "accessor" :signature "(error-object-type e) -> symbol"
  :summary "The condition's NAME as a symbol — the tag `escalate` put on it, or `error` for everything else."
  :explanation "The third accessor on a condition, alongside error-object-message and error-object-irritants, and the only one of the three that is not in R7RS-small. It exists because Motoi has `escalate`, which raises a condition under a NAME you choose — (escalate 'out-of-fuel 3) — and something has to read that name back out in the handler. For a condition raised by escalate, error-object-type returns that symbol. For one raised by `error`, it returns the symbol `error`. Read the next sentence carefully, because it is the difference between a discriminator and a default: error-object-type answers `error` for ANY value that does not carry a type, including a plain number, a string, and a symbol you raised yourself. It never fails and it never says 'I do not know' — so a handler that branches on it cannot distinguish a real error condition from an untyped value that merely arrived through the same channel. Use it to route conditions you yourself named with escalate; do not use it to decide whether a raised value is a condition at all. That question is error-object?'s."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (error-object-type e)) (lambda () (escalate 'over-budget 500)))" :note "over-budget — escalate's first argument comes back as the condition's type.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"       :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (symbol? (error-object-type e))) (lambda () (error \"anything\")))" :note "#t — the answer is always a symbol; a condition from error is typed `error`.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (eq? (error-object-type e) 'outside-domain)) (lambda () (escalate 'outside-domain -1)))" :note "#t — comparing the type with eq? is how a handler decides which condition it is looking at.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(list (error-object-type 42) (error-object-type \"s\") (error-object-type 'sym))" :note "(error error error) — the untyped default. A number is not a condition, and error-object-type says `error` anyway.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"       :code "(define (classify thunk)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list (error-object-type e) (error-object-irritants e))) thunk))\n(display (list (classify (lambda () (escalate 'out-of-fuel 3))) (classify (lambda () (error \"plain\" 7)))))\n(newline)" :note "((out-of-fuel (3)) (error (7))) — type and irritants together are the whole routable payload of a condition."))
  :caveats ("Motoi-own. R7RS-small has error-object?, error-object-message and error-object-irritants and NO error-object-type; portable code cannot use it." "It answers `error` for values that are not conditions at all: (error-object-type 42) is error, and so is (error-object-type \"s\"). It is a total function with a fabricated default, not a discriminator." "A value raised with (raise 'my-signal) reports type `error`, not `my-signal`. Only escalate sets the type." "Engine faults — (car '()), an unbound symbol — also report type `error`, so they are indistinguishable from an explicit (error \"…\") by type alone." "Ask error-object? first if you need to know whether the raised value is a condition; error-object-type will not tell you.")
  :usecases ("Routing a condition raised by escalate to the handler branch that knows about it." "Deciding whether a failure is retryable by comparing its type against a known tag." "Logging the condition's name alongside its message and irritants.")
  :related ("escalate" "error" "error-object?" "error-object-message" "error-object-irritants" "with-exception-handler" "raise")
  :learn (:concept "A condition can carry a NAME as well as a message, and the name is what a handler dispatches on — text is for people, symbols are for code." :prerequisites ("escalate" "with-exception-handler" "eq?") :progression "Raise one named condition with escalate and read its type back, then compare the type with eq? to pick a branch, then build a small table from type to handler.")
  :provenance "Motoi-own; nothing in R7RS-small corresponds. Implementation lib/base/r7rs-small.js:971; measured against the live engine 2026-08-02; reference record added 2026-08-02"
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (route thunk)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (list 'kind (error-object-type e) 'why (error-object-message e))) thunk))\n(display (list (route (lambda () (escalate 'out-of-fuel 3))) (route (lambda () (error \"bad tile\" 9)))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (eq? (error-object-type e) 'outside-domain)) (lambda () (escalate 'outside-domain -1))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (tag-of thunk) (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (error-object-type e)) thunk))\n(display (list (tag-of (lambda () (escalate 'timeout))) (tag-of (lambda () (car '())))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "A cart's handler must react differently to running out of fuel than to a bad tile: one refuels and continues, the other stops. Candidates: match on the message string (works until someone rewords the message, and the reword is invisible to the compiler), put a tag in the irritants and dig it out with car (works, but every raise site must agree on the position), or name the condition with escalate and read the name with error-object-type."
     :explanation "eng: two conditions, one handler, routed by name. math: the type is compared with eq?, which is the exact question — is this THE condition called outside-domain. cs: an engine fault comes back tagged `error`, which is the default, not a name anybody chose."
     :meta "Reach for error-object-type and NOT the message string. A message is prose for a person and is expected to change; a type is a symbol for a program and is expected not to. Routing on prose makes rewording a breaking change.")
    (:eng "The log line carries both: the type for the dashboard to count, the message for the person to read." :math "eq? on an interned symbol is exact equality on the tag set — no substring matching, no near miss." :cs "The `error` answer for (car '()) is the default showing through: the engine never named that fault."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "A failure arrives carrying a name you chose in advance, so the code that catches it can decide what it is without reading English."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "exception" :code "(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (error-object-type e)) (lambda () (escalate 'over-budget 500)))")
       (:domain "a labelled fault light on a machine panel" :code "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (error-object-type e)) (lambda () (escalate 'divide-by-zero))))\n(newline)")
       (:domain "the name of the case in a case analysis" :code "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (symbol? (error-object-type e))) (lambda () (error \"anything\"))))\n(newline)"))
     :explanation "A panel light says WHICH fault, not how the fault feels; a case analysis names its cases so a proof can refer to them; a typed condition names itself so a handler can refer to it. In all three the name is a small closed vocabulary agreed on ahead of time, and the descriptive text hangs off it rather than replacing it."
     :meta "Naming the case is what makes exhaustiveness a question you can ask. Once the tags are a fixed set, 'did I handle them all' has an answer; when failures are distinguished by prose, it does not.")
    (:eng "A closed tag vocabulary is what lets a dashboard count failures by kind instead of by string." :math "The tag partitions the failures into named cases; the case analysis is over the tags, not the messages." :cs "Same shape as a tagged union / sum type discriminant, or an errno. The tag is the thing you switch on."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (eq? 'my-tag (error-object-type e))) (lambda () (escalate 'my-tag))))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(display (list (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (error-object-type e)) (lambda () (error \"m\"))) (error-object-type 42) (error-object-type \"s\")))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (error-object-type e)) (lambda () (raise 'my-signal))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "escalate's name survives the raise verbatim; everything else — a condition from error, a raised bare value, a value that was never raised at all — reports the symbol `error`."
     :invariant "For all symbols s. error-object-type(escalate(s, …)) = s; and for every other value v, error-object-type(v) = 'error"
     :meta "The second and third programs are the ones that matter, and they are unflattering. (error-object-type 42) answers `error` even though 42 is a number that nothing raised, and (raise 'my-signal) reports `error` rather than my-signal. The predicate is total by fabricating a default, so a #t from an eq? test against 'error proves nothing about what was raised. What would falsify the first half of the invariant: an escalate whose name came back changed — it does not."
    )
    (:eng "Only the escalate half of this contract is safe to build on; the default half is a silent yes." :math "Total function, but the totality is bought by mapping the entire complement of the typed conditions to one point." :cs "A discriminator that never returns 'unknown' cannot be used to test membership — that is error-object?'s job, and it is a different question."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (dispatch thunk table)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (let ((hit (assq (error-object-type e) table))) (if hit ((cdr hit) e) 'unhandled))) thunk))\n(display (dispatch (lambda () (escalate 'out-of-fuel 2)) (list (cons 'out-of-fuel (lambda (e) (list 'refuel (error-object-irritants e)))))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (tags thunks) (map (lambda (t) (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (error-object-type e)) t)) thunks))\n(display (tags (list (lambda () (escalate 'a)) (lambda () (escalate 'b)) (lambda () (error \"c\")))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (retry-if tag n thunk)\n  (with-exception-handler (lambda (e) (if (and (eq? (error-object-type e) tag) (> n 0)) (retry-if tag (- n 1) thunk) (list 'stop (error-object-type e)))) thunk))\n(display (retry-if 'flaky 2 (lambda () (escalate 'fatal))))\n(newline)"
     :base "error-object-type reads back the name escalate put on a condition."
     :emergent "eng: a handler TABLE — an alist from type to procedure, so adding a new condition is adding a row rather than editing a cond. math: collecting the types of a batch of thunks, which turns failures into data you can count. cs: a selective retry that only retries the tag it was told to and stops on anything else."
     :meta "The composition that generates all three is type-as-key. Once the failure carries a symbol, every structure that keys on symbols — assq, a hash table, a member test — becomes an exception router for free. The retry is the sharpest use: without a type you must retry everything or nothing, and neither is what an operator wants."
    )
    (:eng "The handler table is the point where an operator can add a recovery without touching the code that fails." :math "Mapping thunks to their failure tags turns a control-flow property into a value you can aggregate." :cs "Selective retry needs a discriminator; this is exactly why the type field exists even though R7RS does not have one."))
)
